Done. All three files are in place and verified.

## Files produced

**`/app/active_users_report.json`** — 346 active users (from 500 input records; `pending` was excluded along with `inactive`), each with `user_id`, `username`, `email`, `last_login` (`YYYY-MM-DD`), `role_count`, `primary_role`, sorted ascending by `username`, indented 2 spaces.

**`/app/cmd.sh`**
```sh
jq --indent 2 '[.[] | select(.status == "active")] | map({user_id: .id, username: .username, email: .email, last_login: (.last_login | .[0:10]), role_count: (.roles | length), primary_role: (.roles[0] // null)}) | sort_by(.username)' /app/users.json > /app/active_users_report.json
```

**`/app/jq_args.yaml`**
```yaml
filter: '[.[] | select(.status == "active")] | map({user_id: .id, username: .username, email: .email, last_login: (.last_login | .[0:10]), role_count: (.roles | length), primary_role: (.roles[0] // null)}) | sort_by(.username)'
args:
  - "--indent 2"
```

## Notes on the approach
- Date formatting uses string slicing (`.last_login | .[0:10]`) rather than `fromdate`, so it is independent of the jq build's `strptime` support — the input format (fractional seconds + `Z`) is fixed and every value matched it.
- `--indent 2` was the only needed jq argument; the filter has no external variables, so `args` holds exactly one entry and `cmd.sh` and the YAML describe the same invocation.
- `primary_role` uses `.roles[0] // null`, which yields real JSON `null` for the 50 active users with empty `roles` (verified: 50 `null` values, zero `"null"` strings).

